{"nodes":[{"id":"mm-intro-01","title":"Introduction to Capital Structure and Modigliani-Miller Theory","description":"This node introduces the concept of capital structure, defining it as the mix of debt and equity used to finance a company's assets. Students will understand the importance of capital structure decisions in corporate finance and be introduced to the foundational Modigliani-Miller (MM) theories. The goal is to provide a comprehensive overview of how these theories challenge conventional wisdom regarding the impact of financing choices on firm value and the cost of capital.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"mm-assumptions-02","title":"Modigliani-Miller Theory: Basic Assumptions","description":"This node delves into the critical assumptions underlying the Modigliani-Miller theories, particularly focusing on the 'perfect capital market' conditions. Students will learn about concepts such as perfect competition, equal access to information, no transaction costs, and no taxes. Other key assumptions like homogeneous business risk classes and homogeneous expectations will also be covered, explaining how these idealized conditions simplify the financial landscape for the initial MM propositions.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"mm-prop1-no-tax-03","title":"MM Proposition I: Firm Value (No Taxes)","description":"This node explains Modigliani-Miller Proposition I under the assumption of no corporate taxes and no bankruptcy costs. Students will learn the core concept that a company's total market value is independent of its capital structure, meaning V(L) = V(U). The node will emphasize that firm value is determined by its operating assets and earnings before interest and taxes (EBIT), not by how it chooses to finance those assets, due to the ability of investors to create homemade leverage.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"mm-prop2-no-tax-04","title":"MM Proposition II: Cost of Capital (No Taxes)","description":"This node focuses on Modigliani-Miller Proposition II in a world without corporate taxes. Students will understand how the overall weighted average cost of capital (WACC) remains constant regardless of the capital structure, despite the use of cheaper debt. It will explain that as leverage increases, the cost of equity (R(E)) rises proportionally to compensate equity holders for the increased financial risk, thereby offsetting the benefit of lower-cost debt and keeping the WACC, R(A), unchanged.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"mm-exercise-no-tax-05","title":"Exercise: Applying MM Propositions I & II (No Taxes)","description":"This exercise challenges students to apply the principles of MM Propositions I and II in a scenario without corporate taxes. Students will calculate firm values and the cost of equity for both unlevered and levered companies, demonstrating their understanding of capital structure irrelevance. The task will involve using the provided formulas to show how WACC remains constant despite changes in the debt-to-equity ratio, reinforcing the theoretical concepts.","type":"exercise","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"mm-prop1-tax-06","title":"MM Theory with Corporate Taxes: Impact on Firm Value","description":"This node introduces the significant impact of corporate taxes on MM theory, moving beyond the 'no tax' assumption. Students will learn how the existence of corporate taxes creates a tax shield for debt, as interest payments are tax-deductible. This tax shield adds value to the levered firm, making the value of a levered firm (V(L)) greater than an unlevered firm (V(U)), specifically by the present value of the interest tax shield.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"mm-prop2-tax-07","title":"MM Theory with Corporate Taxes: Impact on Cost of Capital","description":"This node extends MM Proposition II to incorporate the effect of corporate taxes. Students will understand how the tax deductibility of interest payments effectively lowers the cost of debt for the company. This reduction in the effective cost of debt leads to a decrease in the overall weighted average cost of capital (WACC) as leverage increases, because the tax shield makes debt financing more attractive and valuable to the firm.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"mm-exercise-tax-08","title":"Exercise: Calculating Firm Value and WACC with Corporate Taxes","description":"This exercise requires students to apply MM theory in a scenario where corporate taxes are present. Students will calculate the value of a levered firm, incorporating the present value of the interest tax shield, and determine the WACC for both unlevered and levered companies. This practical application will solidify their understanding of how corporate taxes influence capital structure decisions and the cost of capital, demonstrating the financial benefits of debt in a taxed environment.","type":"exercise","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"mm-quiz-09","title":"Quiz: Modigliani-Miller Theory Fundamentals","description":"This quiz assesses students' comprehensive understanding of the Modigliani-Miller theories, covering both the 'no taxes' and 'with corporate taxes' scenarios. Questions will test their knowledge of basic assumptions, the implications of Proposition I and II on firm value and WACC, and the concept of the interest tax shield. The quiz aims to evaluate their ability to differentiate between the various MM frameworks and apply the core principles.","type":"quiz","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null}]}
