{"nodes":[{"id":"node_1","title":"Defining Capital Structure","description":"This node introduces the fundamental concept of capital structure, defining it as the mix of debt and equity financing a company uses to fund its assets. Students will learn about the two main sources of capital – debt financing and equity financing – and understand that the primary goal is to find the optimal combination that maximizes the company's overall value. We will explore how different proportions of debt and equity can impact a firm's financial health and strategic decisions.","type":"concept","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":"Define capital structure and identify its key components."},{"id":"node_2","title":"Introduction to Financial Leverage","description":"This node delves into the concept of financial leverage, explaining it as the extent to which a company uses debt in its capital structure. Students will learn how leverage is typically measured using the Debt-to-Equity (D/E) ratio, a crucial metric for assessing a company's reliance on borrowed funds. Understanding financial leverage is key to analyzing how a firm can amplify returns to shareholders, but also how it can increase financial risk.","type":"concept","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":"Define financial leverage and explain how it is measured."},{"id":"node_3","title":"Capital Restructuring: Changing Leverage","description":"In this node, we explore capital restructuring, which involves altering a company's leverage without changing its total assets. Students will learn the practical methods by which a company can increase its leverage, such as issuing new debt and using the proceeds to buy back outstanding stock. Conversely, we will also examine how a company can decrease its leverage by issuing new equity and using the funds to repay existing debt, thereby adjusting its risk and return profile.","type":"concept","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":"Describe the process of capital restructuring to increase or decrease leverage."},{"id":"node_4","title":"Financial Management Goals and Optimal Capital Structure","description":"This node outlines the overarching goals of financial management, primarily focusing on maximizing shareholder wealth and firm value, while also minimizing the weighted average cost of capital (WACC). Students will learn that the optimal capital structure is the specific mix of debt and equity that achieves these goals by minimizing the WACC and, consequently, maximizing the company's overall value. We will discuss the interplay between these objectives and how they guide capital structure decisions.","type":"concept","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":"Identify the primary goals of financial management and define optimal capital structure."},{"id":"node_5","title":"Analyzing Financial Leverage Effects: Initial Case Study","description":"This node introduces the Trans Am Corporation as a practical case study to begin analyzing the real-world effects of financial leverage. Students will examine the company's initial financial situation, including its total assets, zero debt, and equity, resulting in a D/E ratio of zero. By understanding this baseline scenario, students will be prepared to later analyze how introducing debt into the capital structure impacts key financial metrics like profitability, earnings per share, and overall risk, setting the stage for deeper analysis.","type":"concept","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":"Analyze an initial company scenario to understand its capital structure before applying leverage."},{"id":"node_6","title":"Quiz: Capital Structure and Leverage Basics","description":"This quiz is designed to assess the student's foundational understanding of the core concepts introduced in the previous nodes. It will cover definitions of capital structure, financial leverage, and capital restructuring, along with the primary objectives of financial management. Successfully completing this quiz will confirm comprehension of essential terminology and principles before progressing to more advanced analytical topics.","type":"quiz","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":"Assess understanding of fundamental capital structure and leverage concepts."}]}
