{"nodes":[{"id":"node_1","title":"Foundations of Capital Structure Theory: MM Propositions","description":"This node will review the evolution of Modigliani-Miller (MM) theory, starting with its initial propositions under perfect market assumptions (Case I). It will then explore how the theory adapts when corporate taxes are introduced (Case II), highlighting the concept and value of the debt tax shield. Finally, it will delve into MM Case III, which incorporates the crucial element of financial distress and bankruptcy costs, providing a comprehensive theoretical framework for understanding capital structure.","type":"concept","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"node_2","title":"The Strategic Impact of Taxation on Capital Structure","description":"This node focuses on the practical implications of taxation in capital structure decisions. Students will learn to evaluate a company's actual tax burden and understand how debt creates a valuable tax shield, reducing the overall cost of capital. We will explore various tax reduction strategies and their effects on a company's financial structure, emphasizing the trade-off between tax benefits and potential financial risks.","type":"concept","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"node_3","title":"Assessing and Mitigating Financial Distress Risk","description":"This node provides a comprehensive guide to evaluating financial crisis risk. It will cover how industry characteristics and a company's operational stability influence its vulnerability to distress. Students will learn to analyze asset liquidity and develop robust cash flow forecasts to predict potential financial challenges, enabling proactive risk management and strategic decision-making.","type":"concept","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"node_4","title":"Practical Considerations for Optimal Capital Structure: Industry & Company-Specific Factors","description":"This node examines the critical factors that guide practical capital structure decisions, focusing on both industry-specific and company-specific considerations. Students will learn why cyclical industries typically favor lower leverage while stable industries can sustain higher debt levels, and how to benchmark against industry averages. Additionally, the node will cover how a company's operating leverage, growth stage, unique funding needs, and management's risk preferences shape its optimal financing mix.","type":"concept","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"node_5","title":"Key Financial Indicators: Monitoring Leverage and Solvency","description":"This node details essential financial ratios used to monitor a company's capital structure and solvency. Students will learn to calculate and interpret leverage-related metrics such as the debt ratio, interest coverage ratio, and fixed charge coverage ratio. Understanding these indicators is crucial for assessing a company's ability to meet its debt obligations and manage its financial risk effectively.","type":"concept","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"node_6","title":"Key Financial Indicators: Monitoring Liquidity and Operational Health","description":"This node focuses on key financial health indicators beyond leverage, specifically those related to liquidity and operational cash flow. Students will learn to calculate and interpret the current ratio and quick ratio to assess short-term liquidity, as well as the operating cash flow ratio to understand a company's ability to generate cash from its core operations. These metrics provide vital insights into a company's overall financial stability and operational efficiency.","type":"concept","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"node_7","title":"Dynamic Capital Structure Management and Strategic Recommendations","description":"This node synthesizes theoretical knowledge with practical implications, emphasizing that no single optimal capital structure fits all companies or situations. Students will learn the importance of maintaining financial flexibility and the necessity of dynamically adjusting the capital structure in response to changing market conditions and internal strategies. The node will also cover establishing early warning mechanisms and developing robust contingency plans to manage financial risks effectively.","type":"concept","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"node_8","title":"Case Study: Applying Capital Structure Management Principles","description":"This exercise-based node will present a realistic business scenario requiring students to apply the concepts learned throughout the unit. Students will analyze a company's financial data, assess its current capital structure, identify key risks and opportunities, and propose strategic adjustments considering tax effects, financial distress risk, industry benchmarks, and company-specific factors. This practical application will solidify their understanding of dynamic capital structure management.","type":"exercise","status":"draft","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null}]}
