","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"unit3-node4","title":"B-Tree Operations: Insertion","description":"This node details the process of inserting a new key into a B-Tree, emphasizing how the tree maintains its balanced structure. Students will learn that insertions always begin at a leaf node. A key aspect is understanding how nodes are split when they become full (exceed 'm' keys), with the median key moving up to the parent node. This upward propagation and splitting process ensures the tree remains balanced and minimizes its depth, which is critical for reducing disk access times.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"unit3-node5","title":"B-Tree Operations: Deletion","description":"This node covers the complexities of deleting a key from a B-Tree while preserving its structural integrity and balance. Students will be introduced to the 'half-full' principle, which dictates that nodes must maintain a minimum number of keys. We will explore various strategies to handle underflow after deletion, such as borrowing keys from sibling nodes, merging nodes, or shifting keys within the tree. These operations are vital to prevent empty nodes and ensure the B-Tree remains efficient and compact.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"unit3-node6","title":"Red-Black Trees: An Order-4 B-Tree Perspective","description":"This node introduces Red-Black Trees as a specific type of self-balancing Binary Search Tree, highlighting their unique relationship to B-Trees. Students will learn to view Red-Black Trees as an Order-4 B-Tree, where 'm=4' implies nodes can have 2-4 branches and 1-3 keys. This perspective helps in understanding their balancing properties, as Red-Black Trees, while implemented as BSTs, effectively mimic the balanced structure and efficiency of a multiway tree, particularly for in-memory operations.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"unit3-node7","title":"Red-Black Tree Balancing: Color Rules and Rotations","description":"This node delves into the specific rules governing Red-Black Trees, which are essential for maintaining their balance. Students will learn about the five key color properties, including the root being black, new nodes being red, and the absence of adjacent red nodes. We will explore how violations of these rules are corrected through a combination of recoloring and tree rotations (left and right rotations). Understanding these mechanisms is crucial for appreciating how Red-Black Trees guarantee efficient search operations with a maximum depth of 2log₂n, making them a robust choice for various applications.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"unit3-node8","title":"Quiz: Search Tree Concepts","description":"This quiz will assess your understanding of the core concepts related to Binary Search Trees, Multiway Search Trees, B-Trees, and Red-Black Trees. Questions will cover definitions, structural properties, operational principles (insertion, deletion), and the unique characteristics that differentiate these tree types. This is an opportunity to solidify your grasp of their utility and underlying mechanics.","type":"quiz","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null}]}
