{"nodes":[{"id":"htc-01","title":"Understanding Hash Table Collisions","description":"This node introduces the fundamental concept of collisions in hash tables, explaining why they occur when two different keys map to the same index. Students will learn that while hash functions aim for uniform distribution, perfect avoidance of collisions is practically impossible. Understanding collisions is the first step towards appreciating the necessity of robust collision resolution strategies to maintain the efficiency and integrity of hash table operations.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"htc-02","title":"Linear Probing: The Simplest Approach","description":"This node delves into Linear Probing, a straightforward collision resolution technique where, upon a collision, the algorithm sequentially searches for the next available slot in the hash table. Students will learn the simple incrementing `(index + 1) % SIZE` formula and understand its ease of implementation. However, the discussion will also critically highlight its primary drawback: the tendency to form 'clusters' of occupied cells, which can significantly degrade performance during insertions and searches.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"htc-03","title":"Exercise: Tracing Linear Probing Operations","description":"In this interactive exercise, students will be tasked with tracing the insertion and search operations in a hash table using Linear Probing for collision resolution. They will practice calculating probe sequences and identifying cluster formation with various key inputs and table sizes. This hands-on activity will reinforce their understanding of the algorithm's mechanics and allow them to observe the effects of clustering in a practical, step-by-step scenario.","type":"exercise","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"htc-04","title":"Quadratic Probing: Mitigating Clustering","description":"This node introduces Quadratic Probing as an advanced open addressing technique designed to mitigate the primary clustering problem inherent in Linear Probing. Students will learn how the probe sequence `(hash(key) + i*i) % SIZE` helps to jump further away from the initial collision point, aiming for a more uniform distribution. The discussion will cover its advantages in reducing primary clustering and its own considerations, such as the potential for secondary clustering and the importance of table size (e.g., prime numbers) to guarantee finding an empty slot.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"htc-05","title":"Chaining: External Collision Resolution","description":"This node explores Chaining, a fundamentally different approach to collision resolution where each slot in the hash table acts as a pointer to a linked list. Upon a collision, the new element is simply added to the linked list at that index, effectively allowing multiple elements to reside at the same hash table index. Students will understand how this method robustly handles a large number of collisions without requiring a larger table or complex probing, but also learn about the trade-off of increased memory overhead due to the pointers in the linked list nodes.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"htc-06","title":"Quiz: Comparing Collision Resolution Techniques","description":"This quiz will assess the student's comprehension of the different collision resolution techniques: Linear Probing, Quadratic Probing, and Chaining. Questions will focus on their respective mechanisms, advantages (e.g., simplicity, reduced clustering, handling many collisions), disadvantages (e.g., clustering, memory overhead), and suitable application scenarios. The goal is to ensure students can differentiate between the methods and understand their practical implications for hash table performance.","type":"quiz","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"htc-07","title":"Trade-offs and Real-World Applications","description":"This node synthesizes the knowledge gained, discussing the critical trade-offs involved in choosing a collision resolution strategy, such as memory usage versus time complexity, and ease of implementation versus performance. It will cover factors like load factor, hash function quality, and the type of data access patterns. Students will explore practical application scenarios, like browser caches, game inventory systems, and programming language symbol tables, to understand why a particular technique might be preferred in different real-world contexts.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null}]}
