":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"DSF004","title":"Inverted Tables: Optimizing Multi-Criteria Searches","description":"This node introduces the concept of Inverted Tables, a specialized data structure often used for efficient multi-criteria sorting and searching in large datasets. Students will learn how Inverted Tables work by creating separate index arrays for different attributes (e.g., sorting by math score, then by English score), allowing for fast retrieval and ordering without re-sorting the primary data. We will analyze their strengths in scenarios requiring rapid filtering and complex queries, and discuss the memory overhead associated with maintaining multiple indices.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"DSF005","title":"Hash Tables: Core Principles and Hash Functions","description":"This node provides a comprehensive introduction to Hash Tables, focusing on their core principle of key-value mapping and O(1) average-case access time. Students will learn about the role of hash functions in converting keys into array indices, the essential requirements for effective hash functions (speed, uniform distribution, consistency), and the concept of collisions. We will discuss why Hash Tables are ideal for fast lookups and insertions, but less suitable for ordered traversal.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"DSF006","title":"Collision Resolution Strategies in Hash Tables","description":"This node examines various strategies for resolving collisions, a fundamental challenge in Hash Table implementation when multiple keys map to the same index. Students will explore common techniques like Linear Probing (simple but prone to clustering), Quadratic Probing (reduces clustering but requires prime table size), and Chaining (handles many collisions but uses extra memory). Each method's advantages, disadvantages, and impact on performance will be thoroughly analyzed.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"DSF007","title":"Performance and Practical Considerations for Hash Tables","description":"This node delves into the critical performance factors and practical considerations for effectively using Hash Tables. Students will learn how the choice of hash function and the appropriate table size significantly impact overall efficiency, differentiating between successful and unsuccessful search performance. We will also cover important operational details, such as the proper handling of data deletion (marking as 'Deleted' instead of 'Empty') to maintain lookup integrity, and common real-world applications like web browser caches and symbol tables.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"DSF008","title":"Comparative Analysis: 2D Array, Jagged Array, Inverted Table, Hash Table","description":"This node synthesizes the knowledge gained by providing a direct comparative analysis of 2D Arrays, Jagged Arrays, Inverted Tables, and Hash Tables. Students will evaluate each structure based on key criteria such as data volume, query frequency, memory constraints, need for sorting, and implementation complexity. The goal is to develop a nuanced understanding of when each data structure excels and its specific trade-offs, solidifying the performance-driven decision framework.","type":"concept","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"DSF009","title":"Scenario-Based Data Structure Selection Exercise","description":"This exercise challenges students to apply the performance-driven decision framework to various real-world scenarios. Students will be presented with different data characteristics and query patterns and asked to justify their choice of data structure (2D Array, Jagged Array, Inverted Table, or Hash Table). This hands-on application will reinforce their understanding of the strengths and weaknesses of each structure and their ability to make optimal design decisions.","type":"exercise","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null},{"id":"DSF010","title":"Unit Quiz: Choosing the Right Data Structure","description":"This quiz assesses the student's mastery of the entire unit, covering the characteristics, applications, and performance considerations of 2D Arrays, Jagged Arrays, Inverted Tables, and Hash Tables. Questions will test their ability to identify the most suitable data structure for given requirements, explain collision resolution techniques, and articulate the trade-offs involved in different choices. The quiz aims to confirm a comprehensive understanding of the decision framework.","type":"quiz","status":"locked","recommended_component":null,"instructional_goal":null}]}
