NODE TITLE: Resolving Hash Collisions: Strategies and Trade-offs
NODE DESC: This node explores various techniques used to resolve collisions in hash tables, which occur when multiple keys map to the same index. Students will learn about open addressing methods like Linear Probing and Quadratic Probing, understanding their advantages (simplicity, reduced clustering) and disadvantages (table size constraints, potential for secondary clustering). Additionally, the node will cover Chaining, a separate chaining method that uses linked lists, discussing its ability to handle high collision rates at the cost of increased memory overhead. Choosing the right collision resolution strategy is vital for maintaining hash table performance.
