{"nodes":[{"id":"structuring-intro","title":"The Need for Structured Data","description":"This node introduces the fundamental reasons why organizing extracted data into a structured format is crucial. Students will learn about the challenges posed by unstructured or semi-structured data, such as difficulty in analysis, querying, and integration. The lesson will highlight the benefits of transforming raw information into a coherent and usable format, emphasizing how structure enhances data accessibility, facilitates automated processing, and supports informed decision-making. Understanding this necessity forms the basis for all subsequent data organization techniques.","status":"locked"},{"id":"tabular-fundamentals","title":"Tabular Data: Rows, Columns, and Headers","description":"This node delves into the core components of tabular data, which is a primary method for organizing extracted information. Students will learn to define and identify rows, which represent individual records (e.g., a single student's complete information), and columns, which represent specific attributes or fields (e.g., 'Name' or 'Student ID Number'). The concept of column headers will be introduced as essential labels for interpreting the data within each column. Using examples from student ID cards, the lesson will clearly illustrate how extracted items map to these tabular elements.","status":"locked"},{"id":"data-fields-types","title":"Identifying Data Fields and Data Types","description":"Building on the concept of columns, this node focuses on the critical process of discerning distinct data elements, known as 'fields,' from extracted information. Students will learn how to systematically categorize these fields (e.g., 'Name,' 'Date of Birth,' 'Department') based on their meaning and purpose. Furthermore, the importance of assigning appropriate data types (e.g., text, numeric, date, boolean) to each field will be covered, explaining how this ensures data integrity, consistency, and compatibility for various processing tasks. Practical exercises using the student ID card data will reinforce field and type identification.","status":"locked"},{"id":"populating-tables","title":"Populating Tables from Extracted Information","description":"This node provides practical guidance on how to transfer raw, extracted data into a predefined structured table. Students will learn the step-by-step process of defining a table schema, which involves establishing consistent column names and their corresponding data types. The lesson will demonstrate how to systematically map and insert values from multiple sources (such as various student ID documents) into the correct rows and columns of the table. The goal is to equip students with the skills to consistently populate a table, ensuring all relevant information is accurately captured and organized for further use.","status":"locked"},{"id":"consistency-usability","title":"Ensuring Data Consistency and Usability","description":"After populating tables, maintaining data quality is paramount. This node addresses the critical aspects of ensuring data consistency and usability within the structured format. Students will explore common issues that arise during extraction and organization, such as inconsistent formatting (e.g., varying date formats), missing values, or potential duplicate entries. The lesson will introduce basic principles for cleaning, standardizing, and validating data to enhance its reliability and readiness for subsequent analysis, reporting, or integration into other systems. The ultimate aim is to make the organized data truly 'usable' for its intended purpose.","status":"locked"}]}
