{"nodes":[{"id":"precision_intro","title":"Introduction to Wafer Fabrication: The Core of Modern Electronics","description":"This node introduces the concept of Integrated Circuits (ICs) as the fundamental component of all modern electronic devices, explaining how millions of transistors are integrated onto a single wafer. It will highlight the 'four high' characteristics of the semiconductor industry: high-tech, high-precision, high-cleanliness, and high-automation. Students will understand why these characteristics are critical for producing advanced computing cores that power everything from smartphones to complex automotive electronics.","status":"locked"},{"id":"cleanroom_imperative","title":"Ultra-Clean Environments: The Foundation of Wafer Yield","description":"This node explores the strategic importance of ultra-clean environments in wafer fabrication, emphasizing why cleanroom standards far exceed those of medical operating rooms. It will detail the rigorous protocols for personnel entry, including metal detection, identity verification, full cleanroom attire changes, and air showers, designed to prevent even the smallest dust particles from contaminating the production area. Students will learn how strict contamination control directly impacts the quality, reliability, and yield of semiconductor products.","status":"locked"},{"id":"advanced_automation","title":"Advanced Automation Systems in Wafer Fabs","description":"This node delves into the critical role of advanced automation in modern wafer fabrication, focusing on systems like Automated Material Handling Systems (AMHS) and Real-Time Dispatching (RTD). It will explain how these technologies enable a production process with minimal human intervention, ensuring optimal efficiency, throughput, and consistent quality across all manufacturing stages. Students will understand how automation maximizes manufacturing capacity, reduces human error, and facilitates complex process coordination.","status":"locked"},{"id":"nanometer_equipment","title":"Nanometer-Scale Precision Equipment and Processes","description":"This node examines the sophisticated machinery and processes that enable nanometer-level precision in wafer manufacturing. It will cover key technologies such as high-temperature thin-film growth furnaces, chemical vapor deposition (CVD), photolithography, etching, and ion implantation. Students will gain insight into how each piece of equipment contributes to the intricate layering and patterning required to build complex integrated circuits with extreme accuracy and reliability.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ic_workflow","title":"The Integrated Circuit Manufacturing Workflow: From Design to Package","description":"This node provides a comprehensive overview of the entire integrated circuit manufacturing workflow, breaking it down into sequential stages. It will cover everything from initial circuit design using Electronic Design Automation (EDA) tools and photomask creation, through wafer manufacturing, thin-film deposition, pattern transfer, etching, ion implantation, and metal connection. Finally, it will touch upon the critical steps of rigorous testing and final packaging, giving students a holistic understanding of the complex journey of an IC.","status":"locked"},{"id":"human_operational_excellence","title":"Operational Excellence and the Human Role in High-Tech Fabs","description":"This node focuses on the human element within highly automated and precise wafer fabrication environments. It will discuss the stringent requirements for on-site personnel, including the need for specialized professional knowledge, unwavering focus, and meticulous adherence to Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs). Students will learn how human responsibility, patience, and strict compliance with cleanroom protocols are indispensable for maintaining product quality and preventing costly errors, even with extensive automation.","status":"locked"},{"id":"capital_intensity","title":