{"nodes":[{"id":"semicon_intro","title":"Semiconductor Industry Overview: The Heart of Modern Tech","description":"This node introduces the fundamental role of Integrated Circuits (ICs) as the core computational engine for all modern electronic devices, from smartphones to AI systems. Students will learn how these tiny, powerful components are manufactured through an incredibly complex, multi-stage process, from circuit design to final packaging. This foundational understanding is crucial for appreciating the industry's strategic importance, its global impact, and the intricate technological marvel behind each chip.","status":"locked"},{"id":"semicon_characteristics","title":"Characteristics of Semiconductor Manufacturing: The 'Four Highs'","description":"Delve into the defining characteristics that make semiconductor manufacturing unique and challenging, often referred to as the 'Four Highs.' This includes the industry's reliance on cutting-edge, high-tech processes involving nanometer-scale precision and advanced automation. We will examine the extreme demands for high cleanliness, far exceeding medical standards, and the pervasive high automation driven by AI and robotics to maximize efficiency and yield. Understanding these characteristics reveals the immense barriers to entry and the specialized expertise required for global competitiveness.","status":"locked"},{"id":"semicon_capital","title":"Capital Intensity and Fixed Asset Dominance","description":"This node explores the staggering capital expenditure required to operate in the semiconductor industry, particularly the high proportion of 'Property, Plant, and Equipment' (PP&E) on company balance sheets. Students will learn why investments in advanced machinery, such as multi-million dollar EUV lithography machines, and the construction of ultra-clean fabrication plants, are exceptionally high. We will also discuss how the rapid pace of technological innovation necessitates frequent equipment upgrades and replacements, further driving capital requirements and shaping strategic financial decisions.","status":"locked"},{"id":"semicon_supplychain","title":"Global Semiconductor Value Chain and Geopolitical Implications","description":"Examine the intricate global supply chain that underpins semiconductor manufacturing, from raw materials to highly specialized equipment and intellectual property. This node will highlight how the multi-country dependencies for each stage of production create potential vulnerabilities and geopolitical leverage points. Students will analyze how disruptions, trade policies, and national security interests can impact the availability and cost of critical components, influencing market strategies and fostering a global 'chessboard' environment.","status":"locked"},{"id":"semicon_strategy","title":"Innovation, Yield, and Market Leadership Strategies","description":"Focus on the continuous drive for innovation and operational excellence that defines market leaders in the semiconductor industry. This node will investigate strategies for improving manufacturing yield, a critical factor in profitability, through advanced technologies and strict process control. We will explore how companies like TSMC maintain their competitive edge by investing heavily in R&D for next-generation technologies (e.g., 3nm, 2nm) and adapting swiftly to evolving market demands and technological breakthroughs to secure market leadership.","status":"locked"}]}
