ws in B). The node will also highlight important properties, such as its non-commutative nature (AB ≠ BA), but its associative ((AB)C = A(BC)) and distributive (A(B+C) = AB + AC) properties, which are essential for advanced matrix algebra.","status":"locked"},{"id":"identity-matrix","title":"The Identity Matrix: The \"One\" of Matrix Algebra","description":"This node introduces the concept of the identity matrix, denoted as I or Iₙ, which plays a role analogous to the number '1' in scalar arithmetic. Students will learn that an identity matrix is a square matrix with ones on its main diagonal and zeros everywhere else. A key property explored is that multiplying any matrix A by the identity matrix I (of appropriate size) results in the original matrix A (AI = IA = A), demonstrating its unique significance in matrix operations.","status":"locked"}]}
