{"nodes":[{"id":"1","title":"Representing Linear Systems with Augmented Matrices","description":"This node introduces learners to systems of linear equations and their compact representation using matrices. Students will learn how to translate a system of multiple linear equations into the standard matrix form Ax = b, where A is the coefficient matrix, x is the variable vector, and b is the constant vector. A key focus will be on constructing the augmented matrix [A|b], which combines the coefficient matrix and the constant vector, setting the stage for systematic solution methods.","status":"locked"},{"id":"2","title":"The Tools: Elementary Row Operations","description":"This node delves into the fundamental operations used to manipulate augmented matrices without altering the solution set of the underlying linear system. Learners will be introduced to the three types of elementary row operations: swapping two rows, multiplying a row by a non-zero scalar, and adding a multiple of one row to another. Understanding these operations is crucial as they form the backbone of Gaussian elimination and other matrix simplification techniques.","status":"locked"},{"id":"3","title":"Gaussian Elimination and Row Echelon Form","description":"This node guides learners through the systematic process of Gaussian elimination, a powerful algorithm for solving linear systems. The primary goal of Gaussian elimination is to transform an augmented matrix into Row Echelon Form (REF) using elementary row operations. Students will learn the step-by-step procedure to create leading ones (pivots) and zeros below them, ensuring the matrix satisfies the characteristics of REF, such as zero rows at the bottom and pivots moving rightward.","status":"locked"},{"id":"4","title":"Interpreting Row Echelon Form: Pivots and Free Variables","description":"Once an augmented matrix is in Row Echelon Form, this node teaches learners how to extract critical information about the system's variables. Students will identify pivot positions, which correspond to leading variables, and non-pivot columns, which indicate free variables. Understanding the distinction between leading and free variables is essential for constructing the solution set and determining the number of solutions a system possesses.","status":"locked"},{"id":"5","title":"Solution Possibilities: Unique, No Solution, Infinite Solutions","description":"Building upon the interpretation of Row Echelon Form, this node explores the three possible outcomes for a system of linear equations: a unique solution, no solution, or infinitely many solutions. Learners will discover how the presence or absence of a pivot in the last column (augmented part) and the existence of free variables directly dictate the nature of the solution set. This node provides clear criteria for classifying system solutions based on the final REF.","status":"locked"},{"id":"6","title":"Homogeneous Systems and General Solutions","description":"This node introduces a special class of linear systems known as homogeneous systems, where the constant vector b is entirely composed of zeros (Ax=0). Students will learn how to find solutions to homogeneous systems, which always have at least the trivial solution (x=0). Furthermore, the node will explain the concept of a general solution for non-homogeneous systems, showing how it is constructed as the sum of a particular solution (x_p) and the solution to the corresponding homogeneous system (x_h).","status":"locked"}]}
