{"nodes":[{"id":"linear-systems-intro","title":"Introduction to Linear Systems and Augmented Matrices","description":"This node introduces the fundamental representation of linear systems as Ax=b, where A is the coefficient matrix, x is the vector of unknowns, and b is the constant vector. We will explore the different possibilities for solutions: unique, no solution, or infinitely many solutions. Furthermore, the concept of an augmented matrix [A|b] will be defined as a compact and efficient way to represent and manipulate linear systems during the solving process, setting the stage for Gaussian elimination.","status":"locked"},{"id":"elementary-row-ops-matrices","title":"Elementary Row Operations and Elementary Matrices","description":"This section defines the three fundamental elementary row operations: swapping two rows, multiplying a row by a non-zero scalar, and adding a multiple of one row to another. We will then introduce elementary matrices, which are obtained by performing a single elementary row operation on an identity matrix. Understanding these matrices is crucial, as any elementary row operation can be represented by pre-multiplication by an elementary matrix, and we will examine their key property of being invertible.","status":"locked"},{"id":"gaussian-elimination-ref","title":"Gaussian Elimination: The Path to Row Echelon Form","description":"This node delves into the systematic process of Gaussian elimination, a cornerstone algorithm for solving linear systems and transforming matrices. The primary objective is to convert a given matrix into row echelon form (REF) using a sequence of elementary row operations. We will define a 'pivot' as the first non-zero entry in a non-zero row and illustrate how to strategically use row operations to create zeros below each pivot, moving towards the desired triangular-like structure.","status":"locked"},{"id":"row-echelon-form-properties","title":"Row Echelon Form (REF) and Its Rigorous Properties","description":"This node provides a rigorous definition of Row Echelon Form (REF), detailing its essential characteristics. These properties include: all zero rows must be at the bottom of the matrix, the leading entry (pivot) of each non-zero row must be to the right of the leading entry of the row immediately above it, and all entries in a column below a leading entry must be zero. Mastering these characteristics is fundamental for correctly applying Gaussian elimination and interpreting its results.","status":"locked"},{"id":"solving-systems-ref","title":"Solving Linear Systems from Row Echelon Form","description":"Building upon the concept of Row Echelon Form, this node explains how to extract solutions from a system transformed by Gaussian elimination. We will learn to identify pivot variables and free variables based on the positions of the pivots. This distinction is critical for determining whether a system has a unique solution, no solution, or infinitely many solutions, and for constructing the general solution, including particular solutions and solutions to the associated homogeneous system (Ax=0).","status":"locked"},{"id":"intro-matrix-decompositions-lu","title":"Introduction to Matrix Decompositions and LU Decomposition","description":"This node introduces the powerful concept of matrix decomposition, explaining its benefits, particularly in efficiently solving multiple linear systems with the same coefficient matrix. We will then focus on LU decomposition, where a matrix A is factored into the product of a lower triangular matrix L and an upper triangular matrix U (A=LU). The conditions under which a matrix can be directly decomposed into LU form without requiring row permutations will be thoroughly discussed.","status":"locked"},{"id":"solving-systems-lu","title":"Solving Systems Using LU Decomposition","description":"This node demonstrates the practical application of LU decomposition for solving linear systems of the form Ax=b. The method involves a two-step process: first, solving the lower triangular system Ly=b for the intermediate vector y using forward substitution. Subsequently, the upper triangular system Ux=y is solved for x using backward substitution. This approach significantly enhances computational efficiency compared to repeatedly performing Gaussian elimination for each new right-hand side vector b.","status":"locked"},{"id":"permutation-matrices","title":"Permutation Matrices and Their Essential Role","description":"This node thoroughly defines permutation matrices as square binary matrices with exactly one entry of 1 in each row and each column, and zeros elsewhere. We will explore their unique properties, notably that their inverse is equal to their transpose (P⁻¹=Pᵀ). Critically, we will understand how permutation matrices are used to perform row interchanges, which are often necessary during Gaussian elimination to ensure the existence of certain matrix decompositions.","status":"locked"},{"id":"plu-decomposition-general","title":"PLU Decomposition: The General Case for Solving Systems","description":"This node introduces the more robust PLU decomposition, which factors a matrix A into the product of a permutation matrix P, a lower triangular matrix L, and an upper triangular matrix U (A=PLU). Unlike simple LU decomposition, PLU decomposition is applicable to any invertible matrix, as the permutation matrix P accounts for any necessary row interchanges during the Gaussian elimination process. This generalization is vital for solving a broader range of linear systems reliably.","status":"locked"},{"id":"solving-systems-plu","title":"Solving Systems Using PLU Decomposition","description":"This node details the comprehensive procedure for solving linear systems Ax=b using the versatile PLU decomposition. The process begins by applying the permutation matrix P to both sides of the equation, transforming it into PAx=Pb. Substituting the decomposition, we get LUx=Pb. This transformed system is then solved in two efficient steps: first, Ly=Pb for the intermediate vector y using forward substitution, and then Ux=y for the final solution vector x using backward substitution, demonstrating its full power.","status":"locked"}]}
