{"nodes":[{"id":"linear-dependence-independence","title":"Defining Linear Dependence and Independence","description":"This node introduces the fundamental concepts of linear dependence and independence among a set of vectors. Students will learn the precise definitions, understanding that a set of vectors is linearly dependent if a non-trivial linear combination yields the zero vector, and linearly independent otherwise. The node will also explain how to test for linear dependence/independence by setting up and solving a homogeneous linear system (Ax = 0), where the columns of A are the vectors in question. This understanding is crucial for analyzing the structure of solution spaces and matrix properties.","status":"locked"},{"id":"homogeneous-systems","title":"Analyzing Homogeneous Linear Systems (Ax=0)","description":"This node focuses on homogeneous linear systems, represented by the equation Ax = 0. Students will explore the nature of their solution sets, distinguishing between the trivial solution (x = 0) and non-trivial solutions. The existence of non-trivial solutions will be directly linked to the linear dependence of the column vectors of A. This section will lay the groundwork for understanding the null space of a matrix and its connection to free variables, which dictate the structure of infinite solutions.","status":"locked"},{"id":"non-homogeneous-systems","title":"Solving Non-Homogeneous Linear Systems (Ax=b)","description":"Building upon the understanding of homogeneous systems, this node delves into non-homogeneous linear systems, Ax = b, where b is a non-zero vector. Students will learn how the general solution to Ax = b is expressed as the sum of a particular solution (xₚ) and the general solution to the corresponding homogeneous system (xₕ). This decomposition highlights the relationship between the two types of systems and provides a comprehensive framework for finding all possible solutions when they exist, emphasizing the role of the homogeneous solution in describing the 'shape' of the solution space.","status":"locked"},{"id":"pivots-free-variables","title":"Understanding Pivots and Free Variables","description":"This node explains the critical concepts of pivots and free variables, which emerge during Gaussian elimination when transforming a matrix into row echelon form. Students will learn to identify pivot columns and their corresponding variables, as well as non-pivot columns and their associated free variables. The presence and number of free variables directly determine whether a system has a unique solution, no solution, or infinitely many solutions, providing a powerful tool for characterizing the solution space of any linear system. This understanding is fundamental for constructing the general solution, especially in cases with infinite solutions.","status":"locked"},{"id":"intro-matrix-decompositions","title":"Overview of Matrix Decompositions","description":"This node provides a general introduction to the concept of matrix decompositions, explaining why matrices are broken down into products of simpler matrices. Students will understand that decompositions like LU, PLU, and LDU are not just mathematical curiosities but powerful tools for simplifying computations, solving linear systems more efficiently, and revealing underlying structural properties of matrices. The node sets the stage for a detailed exploration of LDU decomposition by highlighting its advantages and applications in various numerical algorithms.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ldu-derivation","title":"Deriving and Analyzing LDU Decomposition","description":"This node provides a detailed derivation of the LDU decomposition, where a matrix A is factored into A = LDU. Students will learn that L is a lower triangular matrix with ones on its diagonal, U is an upper triangular matrix also with ones on its diagonal, and D is a diagonal matrix containing the pivots. The derivation will emphasize the process of using elementary row operations to systematically transform A into U, while simultaneously constructing L and D. This decomposition offers a more refined factorization than LU, clearly separating the scaling factors into the diagonal matrix D.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ldu-properties-applications","title":"Properties and Applications of LDU Decomposition","description":"This node explores the key properties and practical applications of the LDU decomposition. Students will learn about its uniqueness (when it exists) and its particular significance for symmetric matrices, where L is the transpose of U (A = LDLᵀ). The node will discuss how LDU decomposition simplifies the solution of linear systems, facilitates the calculation of determinants, and provides insights into the stability of numerical methods. Understanding these applications reinforces the value of this advanced matrix factorization in various fields.","status":"locked"},{"id":"matrix-invertibility-elementary","title":"Matrix Invertibility and Elementary Matrices","description":"This node establishes the crucial link between matrix invertibility and elementary matrices. Students will understand that an invertible matrix can be expressed as a product of elementary matrices, and conversely, any product of elementary matrices is invertible. The node will review the process of finding an inverse using Gaussian elimination on the augmented matrix [A|I] and connect the ability to reduce A to the identity matrix to its invertibility. This section also reinforces the idea that invertible matrices have linearly independent columns and rows.","status":"locked"},{"id":"fundamental-theorems","title":"Key Theorems: Invertibility, Linear Independence, and Solutions","description":"This node synthesizes several fundamental theorems connecting matrix invertibility, linear independence of column vectors, and the nature of solutions to linear systems. Students will learn that for an n x n matrix, invertibility is equivalent to its column vectors being linearly independent, to the homogeneous system Ax=0 having only the trivial solution, and to Ax=b having a unique solution for every b. The node will also cover properties like the invertibility of products of invertible matrices (e.g., (AB)⁻¹ = B⁻¹A⁻¹) and how these theorems provide a comprehensive understanding of matrix behavior.","status":"locked"}]}
