{"nodes":[{"id":"eigen_01","title":"Defining Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors","description":"This node introduces the fundamental definitions of eigenvalues and eigenvectors. Students will learn about the core equation Ax = λx, where A is a square matrix, x is an eigenvector, and λ is its corresponding eigenvalue. We will explain that an eigenvector is a non-zero vector that, when transformed by a given matrix, only scales without changing its direction. The eigenvalue represents this scaling factor.","status":"locked"},{"id":"eigen_02","title":"Geometric Significance","description":"This node delves into the geometric interpretation of eigenvalues and eigenvectors. Students will understand that eigenvectors represent the 'invariant directions' or principal axes of a linear transformation, meaning they are the directions that remain unchanged after the transformation, only being stretched or compressed. The eigenvalue quantifies this stretching or compression, indicating how much the vector is scaled along its invariant direction. This visual understanding is crucial for grasping their importance in various applications.","status":"locked"},{"id":"eigen_03","title":"Calculating Eigenvalues: The Characteristic Equation","description":"This node guides students through the first step of finding eigenvalues. We will derive the characteristic equation, det(A - λI) = 0, which is obtained by setting the determinant of (A - λI) to zero, ensuring non-trivial solutions for x. Students will learn how to set up this equation for a given matrix A and solve the resulting polynomial equation to find the eigenvalues (λ values). This involves understanding determinants and solving polynomial roots.","status":"locked"},{"id":"eigen_04","title":"Calculating Eigenvectors: Solving (A - λI)x = 0","description":"Once eigenvalues are found, this node focuses on calculating their corresponding eigenvectors. For each eigenvalue λ, students will learn to substitute it back into the equation (A - λI)x = 0 and solve the homogeneous system of linear equations. This process involves techniques like Gaussian elimination to find the null space of the matrix (A - λI), which represents the eigenspace spanned by the eigenvectors. We will emphasize that eigenvectors are directions, so any non-zero scalar multiple of a found eigenvector is also an eigenvector for that eigenvalue.","status":"locked"},{"id":"eigen_05","title":"Properties and Common Pitfalls","description":"This node covers important properties of eigenvalues and eigenvectors, such as the fact that eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent. We will also discuss common calculation errors, like sign errors in determinants or polynomial roots, and emphasize verification methods by plugging calculated values back into Ax = λx. The concept of eigenspace and its dimension will also be introduced, laying groundwork for more advanced topics.","status":"locked"}]}
