{"nodes":[{"id":"repeated-eigenvalues-diagonalization","title":"Repeated Eigenvalues and Matrix Diagonalization","description":"This node delves into the complexities of matrices with repeated eigenvalues, distinguishing between algebraic multiplicity (the root's multiplicity in the characteristic polynomial) and geometric multiplicity (the dimension of the corresponding eigenspace). Students will learn that a matrix is diagonalizable if and only if the algebraic multiplicity of every eigenvalue equals its geometric multiplicity. We will also explore cases where a matrix is not diagonalizable, introducing the concept of Jordan blocks as a canonical form for such matrices. Understanding these distinctions is crucial for advanced matrix analysis and solving linear systems.","status":"locked"},{"id":"complex-eigenvalues-implications","title":"Complex Eigenvalues and Associated Eigenvectors","description":"This section addresses the occurrence of complex eigenvalues in real matrices, a common phenomenon in various applications. Students will discover that complex eigenvalues always appear in conjugate pairs, and their corresponding eigenvectors are also complex conjugates. We will explore how to compute these complex eigenvalues and eigenvectors, and discuss their significance in understanding transformations that involve rotation or oscillation. This topic extends the scope of eigenvalue analysis beyond real numbers, providing a more complete picture of matrix behavior.","status":"locked"},{"id":"matrix-powers-dynamic-systems","title":"Matrix Powers and Dynamic System Stability","description":"This node explores the practical utility of eigenvalues in two key areas. First, we will learn how diagonalization significantly simplifies matrix power calculations, enabling efficient computation of A^n using the formula PD^nP^(-1). Second, we will apply eigenvalue theory to analyze the stability and long-term behavior of dynamic systems. Students will understand how the real and imaginary parts of eigenvalues determine whether a system converges (negative real part), diverges (positive real part), or oscillates (pure imaginary part), providing critical insights into system dynamics.","status":"locked"},{"id":"advanced-applications-pca-svd","title":"Advanced Applications: PCA, SVD, and Positive Definite Matrices","description":"This final node broadens the scope of matrix theory by introducing its fundamental applications in data science and numerical analysis. Students will learn about Principal Component Analysis (PCA), a powerful dimensionality reduction technique that relies on eigenvalue decomposition to identify the most significant features in data. We will also explore Singular Value Decomposition (SVD), a generalization of eigenvalue decomposition applicable to any matrix, which has wide-ranging uses from image compression to recommender systems. Furthermore, the relationship between eigenvalues and positive definite matrices will be discussed, highlighting their importance in optimization and quadratic forms.","status":"locked"}]}
