{"nodes":[{"id":"inverse_gauss_elim","title":"Finding Inverse Matrices via Gaussian Elimination","description":"This node will teach learners the primary method for computing the inverse of a square matrix using Gaussian elimination. We will transform the augmented matrix [A|I] into [I|A⁻¹] through a series of elementary row operations. Understanding this process is crucial for not only finding inverses but also for comprehending the underlying mechanics of matrix invertibility. The node will cover the step-by-step procedure and discuss conditions under which a matrix is not invertible.","status":"locked"},{"id":"elem_perm_matrices","title":"Elementary and Permutation Matrices","description":"Learners will explore elementary matrices, which are formed by performing a single elementary row operation on an identity matrix. We will categorize the three types of elementary matrices (row swap, row scaling, row addition) and understand their role in matrix multiplication. Additionally, the node will introduce permutation matrices, which are special elementary matrices used for row exchanges, and highlight their unique property where P⁻¹ = Pᵀ. These concepts are fundamental for understanding matrix decompositions and the invertibility of matrices.","status":"locked"},{"id":"linear_dependence_independence","title":"Linear Dependence and Independence of Vectors","description":"This node focuses on the critical concepts of linear dependence and independence among a set of vectors. Learners will define these terms, understanding that a set of vectors is linearly dependent if a non-trivial linear combination results in the zero vector. We will learn how to determine the linear dependence or independence of vectors by solving the homogeneous system Ax = 0, where the columns of A are the vectors in question. This concept is foundational for understanding vector spaces, bases, and matrix rank.","status":"locked"},{"id":"homogeneous_systems","title":"Solutions to Homogeneous Linear Systems (Ax = 0)","description":"This node delves into homogeneous linear systems, which are expressed in the form Ax = 0. Learners will discover that such systems always have at least the trivial solution (x = 0). We will explore conditions under which a homogeneous system has non-trivial solutions, specifically when there are free variables in the row echelon form of A. Understanding the structure of these solutions (the null space of A) is vital for grasping the concept of linear dependence and the general solution to non-homogeneous systems.","status":"locked"},{"id":"non_homogeneous_systems","title":"Solutions to Non-Homogeneous Linear Systems (Ax = b)","description":"Learners will study non-homogeneous linear systems, represented as Ax = b where b is a non-zero vector. This node will explain the three possibilities for solutions: a unique solution, no solution, or infinitely many solutions. A key focus will be on understanding the structure of the general solution, which is given by x = xₚ + xₕ, where xₚ is a particular solution to Ax = b and xₕ is the general solution to the associated homogeneous system Ax = 0. We will also discuss the role of free variables in determining the nature of these solutions.","status":"locked"},{"id":"invertible_matrix_theorem","title":"Key Properties and Theorems of Invertible Matrices","description":"This node consolidates various important properties and theorems related to invertible matrices. Learners will understand that if a matrix is invertible, its column vectors are linearly independent. We will explore theorems such as the invertibility of a product of matrices, (AB)⁻¹ = B⁻¹A⁻¹, and how invertible matrices relate to unique solutions of linear systems. This node aims to provide a comprehensive understanding of what it means for a matrix to be invertible and the far-reaching implications of this property across linear algebra.","status":"locked"}]}
