{"nodes":[{"id":"lp_intro","title":"Introduction to Linear Programming Models","description":"This node introduces the fundamental concepts of Linear Programming (LP), explaining its purpose as a powerful mathematical technique for optimizing resource allocation under constraints. Students will learn about the key components of an LP model: decision variables, the objective function, and constraints. It will cover scenarios where LP is applicable in business and management, setting the stage for translating real-world problems into a structured mathematical format.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_variables","title":"Identifying Decision Variables","description":"Understanding how to correctly define decision variables is the first critical step in formulating an LP model. This node focuses on identifying the unknown quantities that need to be determined to solve a problem, such as the amount of products to produce or resources to allocate. Students will learn how to clearly define these variables, including their units and what they represent in the context of a business problem, ensuring they are unambiguous and relevant to the optimization goal.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_objective","title":"Crafting the Objective Function","description":"The objective function defines the goal of the optimization problem, whether it's to maximize profit or minimize cost. This node teaches students how to formulate a linear objective function by identifying the relevant coefficients (e.g., profit per unit, cost per unit) associated with each decision variable. It emphasizes ensuring the objective function accurately reflects the problem's aim and is expressed as a linear combination of the decision variables.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_constraints_res_dem","title":"Formulating Resource and Demand Constraints","description":"Constraints represent the limitations or requirements within the problem, such as available resources or demand levels. This node guides students through the process of translating real-world restrictions into linear inequalities or equalities. It specifically focuses on common types of constraints related to resource availability (e.g., labor hours, raw materials) and meeting specific demand targets, teaching how to express these relationships mathematically using the defined decision variables.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_constraints_special","title":"Formulating Special Case Constraints and Non-Negativity","description":"Beyond basic resource and demand limits, many business problems involve specific constraints like production capacity, blending ratios, or minimum quality standards. This node explores how to formulate these more specialized constraints, ensuring all operational rules are captured. It also introduces the crucial concept of non-negativity constraints, which stipulate that decision variables cannot take negative values, a common and essential requirement in most practical LP models.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_full_model","title":"Building a Complete LP Model: A Step-by-Step Approach","description":"This node synthesizes all the previously learned components into a coherent, complete Linear Programming model. Students will follow a systematic approach to define variables, construct the objective function, and formulate all relevant constraints, including non-negativity. The focus is on ensuring the model accurately represents the real-world problem, is mathematically sound, and is ready for solution using LP techniques, providing a holistic view of the formulation process.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_case_production","title":"Case Study: Production Planning Problem","description":"This node applies the learned LP formulation steps to a classic production planning scenario. Students will work through a detailed example involving multiple products, limited resources (e.g., labor, machine time, raw materials), and potentially demand considerations. The goal is to maximize profit or minimize cost by determining optimal production quantities, reinforcing the practical application of defining variables, objective functions, and various constraints in a complex business context.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_case_diet_blending","title":"Case Study: Diet and Blending Problems","description":"This node delves into another common application of LP: diet and blending problems, which involve mixing ingredients to meet specific nutritional or quality requirements at minimum cost. Students will analyze scenarios like formulating animal feed, creating a food product with specific nutrient profiles, or blending raw materials to achieve a desired output. This case study will solidify the understanding of how to set up constraints for minimum/maximum requirements and ingredient proportions, along with cost minimization objectives.","status":"locked"}]}
