ations or restrictions on the resources available or the requirements that must be met. Students will learn how to translate real-world limitations into linear mathematical inequalities or equalities involving the decision variables. Understanding constraints is vital as they define the feasible region within which the optimal solution must lie, ensuring that the chosen solution is practical and adheres to all operational boundaries.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_intro_5","title":"Key Assumptions of Linear Programming","description":"This node covers the fundamental assumptions underpinning Linear Programming, including linearity, divisibility, certainty, and non-negativity. Students will explore what each assumption means and its implications for modeling real-world problems using LP. Understanding these assumptions is critical for determining when LP is an appropriate tool and for interpreting the validity and limitations of the solutions obtained, ensuring a practical application of the technique.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_intro_6","title":"Relevance and Applications in Business","description":"This node highlights the practical relevance of Linear Programming by exploring its diverse applications across various business functions. Students will learn how LP is used in areas such as production planning, resource allocation, supply chain management, financial portfolio optimization, and marketing mix decisions. Through examples, this node will demonstrate how LP provides a structured approach to making optimal decisions, leading to increased efficiency, reduced costs, and improved profitability for organizations.","status":"locked"}]}
