{"nodes":[{"id":"ODFI01","title":"Understanding Raw Data and the Need for Organization","description":"This node introduces the concept of raw data, which is data collected in its original, unorganized form. Students will learn why it is crucial to organize raw data to extract meaningful insights, identify patterns, and make informed decisions. We will discuss the challenges presented by unorganized data and the fundamental benefits of structuring it for analysis. This initial understanding sets the stage for the systematic methods to be covered in subsequent nodes, highlighting the importance of data preparation in any analytical process.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ODFI02","title":"Frequency Distributions for Categorical Data","description":"This node focuses on systematic methods for organizing qualitative or categorical data. Students will learn how to construct frequency distributions, which show the count of occurrences for each distinct category. We will also cover relative frequency distributions, expressing each category's proportion of the total observations, and percentage frequency distributions, which convert these proportions into percentages. Understanding these distributions is fundamental for summarizing and interpreting non-numerical information effectively, allowing for quick comparisons between categories.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ODFI03","title":"Grouping Numerical Data into Frequency Distributions","description":"This node delves into organizing quantitative or numerical data, which often spans a wide range of values. Students will learn the process of grouping numerical data into classes or intervals to create a frequency distribution. Key steps include determining the appropriate number of classes, calculating the class width, and defining clear class limits to avoid ambiguity. This method transforms a large, unwieldy dataset into a more manageable and interpretable summary, revealing the shape and spread of the data's distribution.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ODFI04","title":"Relative and Cumulative Frequency Distributions for Numerical Data","description":"Building upon the concept of grouped numerical data, this node explores more advanced distribution types for quantitative information. Students will learn to construct relative frequency distributions for numerical data, showing the proportion of observations within each defined class interval. Furthermore, we will introduce cumulative frequency distributions, which indicate the total number or proportion of observations falling below the upper limit of each successive class. These distributions provide deeper insights into data accumulation, percentile ranks, and the overall spread of values within a dataset.","status":"locked"}]}
