{"nodes":[{"id":"UDV-01","title":"What is Data?","description":"This node introduces the fundamental concept of data, defining it as raw facts, figures, or information collected for analysis. Students will learn why data is crucial in decision-making across various fields, especially in business and management. We will explore common sources of data and the initial steps involved in data collection, setting the foundation for understanding its subsequent classification and analysis.","status":"locked"},{"id":"UDV-02","title":"Differentiating Data: Qualitative vs. Quantitative","description":"This node focuses on the primary distinction between qualitative and quantitative data. Students will learn to identify qualitative data, which describes characteristics or categories, and quantitative data, which deals with numerical values and measurements. We will discuss examples of each type and the inherent differences in how they are collected, represented, and initially interpreted, preparing students for deeper dives into specific measurement scales.","status":"locked"},{"id":"UDV-03","title":"Qualitative Data: Nominal and Ordinal Scales","description":"This node delves into the two main measurement scales for qualitative data: nominal and ordinal. Students will understand that nominal data categorizes without any inherent order, while ordinal data categorizes with a meaningful rank or order but without precise differences between values. We will provide clear examples for each scale and discuss their characteristics, highlighting the types of analyses that are appropriate or inappropriate for data measured at these levels.","status":"locked"},{"id":"UDV-04","title":"Quantitative Data: Interval and Ratio Scales","description":"This node explores the two higher-level measurement scales for quantitative data: interval and ratio. Students will learn that interval data has ordered categories with meaningful differences between values, but no true zero point, while ratio data possesses all the characteristics of interval data plus a meaningful absolute zero. We will illustrate these scales with practical examples and explain how the presence or absence of a true zero impacts the validity of certain comparisons and statistical operations.","status":"locked"},{"id":"UDV-05","title":"Understanding Variables: Types and Roles","description":"This node bridges the concept of data types to variables, defining a variable as a characteristic or attribute that can take on different values. Students will learn about independent and dependent variables, and how the type of data (qualitative or quantitative) dictates the type of variable. We will discuss the importance of clearly defining variables in research and analysis, emphasizing how this understanding is critical for formulating research questions and hypotheses.","status":"locked"},{"id":"UDV-06","title":"Data Types and Scales: Implications for Analysis","description":"This concluding node synthesizes the knowledge of data types and measurement scales, explaining their critical implications for statistical analysis. Students will understand why choosing the correct statistical method depends heavily on the scale of measurement of the variables involved. We will discuss how different scales limit or enable specific mathematical operations and statistical tests, ensuring students can make informed decisions about appropriate analytical techniques for various data scenarios.","status":"locked"}]}
