{"nodes":[{"id":"lp_intro_1","title":"What is Linear Programming?","description":"This node introduces the fundamental concept of Linear Programming (LP), explaining it as a mathematical technique used to optimize a linear objective function, such as maximizing profit or minimizing cost, subject to a set of linear constraints. Students will learn the core idea behind LP: making the best possible decision when faced with limited resources and specific requirements. We will cover why LP is a powerful tool for decision-making in various business and management contexts, setting the stage for more detailed exploration.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_components_1","title":"Decision Variables and Objective Function","description":"This node delves into two critical components of any Linear Programming model: Decision Variables and the Objective Function. Students will learn how to identify and define decision variables, which represent the choices or quantities that need to be determined to solve a problem. Furthermore, it will explain how to formulate the objective function, a linear mathematical expression that represents the goal (e.g., maximizing profit, minimizing cost, or maximizing utility) that the LP model aims to achieve, using these decision variables.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_components_2","title":"Constraints in LP Models","description":"Building on the previous node, this section focuses on understanding and formulating constraints, the limitations or requirements that must be satisfied in an LP problem. Students will learn about various types of constraints, such as resource availability, demand limits, production capacities, and policy restrictions, all expressed as linear inequalities or equalities. A key aspect will be understanding the non-negativity constraint, ensuring that decision variables typically represent non-negative quantities, and how to translate real-world limitations into precise mathematical expressions.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_formulation","title":"Formulating a Complete LP Problem","description":"This node integrates all the previously learned components to demonstrate how to formulate a complete Linear Programming problem from a real-world scenario. Students will practice the systematic process of identifying decision variables, defining the objective function, and translating all relevant constraints into a concise mathematical model. This skill is crucial for applying LP to practical problems, enabling students to transform descriptive business challenges into solvable mathematical structures ready for optimization.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lp_applications","title":"Practical Applications of Linear Programming","description":"This node explores the wide array of practical applications of Linear Programming across various industries and functional areas of business and management. Students will be introduced to real-world examples such as production planning, resource allocation, transportation problems, diet formulation, financial portfolio optimization, and marketing mix decisions. The aim is to illustrate how LP provides optimal solutions to complex problems involving resource scarcity and competing objectives, showcasing its immense value as a decision-making tool.","status":"locked"}]}
