{"nodes":[{"id":"LP_GRAPH_01","title":"Introduction to Linear Programming & Graphical Method Overview","description":"This node introduces the fundamental concepts of Linear Programming (LP), defining its core components such as decision variables, objective functions, and constraints. Students will learn what constitutes an LP problem and why it's a powerful tool for optimization. We will also provide an overview of the graphical method, highlighting its applicability for two-variable problems and setting the stage for visual problem-solving.","status":"locked"},{"id":"LP_GRAPH_02","title":"Graphing Linear Inequalities and Constraint Lines","description":"This node focuses on the essential skill of translating linear inequalities into visual representations on a Cartesian plane. Students will learn how to plot individual constraint lines by converting inequalities to equalities. Furthermore, they will master identifying and shading the correct region that satisfies each inequality, including critical non-negativity constraints, which form the boundaries of the solution space.","status":"locked"},{"id":"LP_GRAPH_03","title":"Defining the Feasible Region","description":"Building upon graphing individual inequalities, this node teaches students how to combine multiple linear constraints on a single graph to identify the feasible region. The feasible region represents the set of all possible combinations of decision variables that satisfy every constraint simultaneously. Students will learn techniques to accurately pinpoint this region and identify its vertices, which are critical for finding the optimal solution.","status":"locked"},{"id":"LP_GRAPH_04","title":"The Objective Function and Isoprofit/Isocost Lines","description":"This node introduces the objective function, which students aim to either maximize or minimize. It explains how to represent this function graphically using 'isoprofit' lines for maximization problems or 'isocost' lines for minimization problems. Students will learn to draw these lines and understand that moving them parallel to themselves across the feasible region helps determine the direction of improvement and identify potential optimal points.","status":"locked"},{"id":"LP_GRAPH_05","title":"Identifying the Optimal Solution (Corner Point Method)","description":"This node delves into the corner point method, a systematic approach for finding the optimal solution in a graphical LP problem. Students will learn that the optimal solution (maximum or minimum value of the objective function) for a linear programming problem always occurs at one of the vertices (corner points) of the feasible region. The process involves evaluating the objective function at each vertex to determine the best possible outcome.","status":"locked"},{"id":"LP_GRAPH_06","title":"Special Cases in Graphical LP","description":"This node explores various special scenarios that can arise when solving linear programming problems graphically. Students will learn to identify and interpret cases such as no feasible solution (where no points satisfy all constraints), unbounded solutions (where the objective function can be infinitely improved), and multiple optimal solutions (where an entire segment of the feasible region boundary yields the same optimal value). Understanding these exceptions provides a more complete grasp of LP outcomes.","status":"locked"},{"id":"LP_GRAPH_07","title":"Complete Graphical Solution Walkthrough","description":"This concluding node consolidates all previously learned concepts through a comprehensive step-by-step example. Students will follow a complete graphical solution process, from formulating an LP problem to plotting constraints, identifying the feasible region, and applying either the isoprofit/isocost line method or the corner point method to find the optimal solution. This practical application node aims to solidify understanding and build confidence in solving two-variable LP problems graphically.","status":"locked"}]}
