{"nodes":[{"id":"simplex_intro_standard_form","title":"Introduction to the Simplex Method and Standard Form","description":"This node introduces the necessity of the Simplex method as an algebraic procedure for solving linear programming problems with more than two variables, where graphical methods become impractical. Students will learn the fundamental requirements for applying the Simplex method, specifically how to convert any linear programming problem into its 'standard form'. This involves ensuring all constraints are equalities, all variables are non-negative, and the objective function is either maximized or minimized by introducing slack and surplus variables.","status":"locked"},{"id":"basic_feasible_solutions_tableau","title":"Basic Feasible Solutions and Initial Simplex Tableau","description":"This node delves into the concept of Basic Feasible Solutions (BFS), which are the algebraic equivalent of corner points in the feasible region. Students will understand how to identify basic and non-basic variables within the standard form of an LP and how these relate to the number of variables and constraints. The core learning involves setting up the initial Simplex tableau, which is a tabular representation of the system of equations, and identifying the initial basic feasible solution from this setup, preparing for the iterative process.","status":"locked"},{"id":"simplex_algorithm_pivot_selection","title":"The Simplex Algorithm: Entering and Leaving Variables","description":"This node focuses on the first critical steps of the iterative Simplex algorithm: selecting which variables enter and leave the basis. Students will learn how to choose the 'entering variable' by identifying the pivot column, typically based on the most negative coefficient in the objective row for maximization problems (optimality condition). Subsequently, they will master the 'minimum ratio test' to determine the 'leaving variable' and thus the pivot row, ensuring that feasibility is maintained in the next iteration.","status":"locked"},{"id":"pivoting_operations_iteration","title":"Pivoting Operations and Iteration to Optimality","description":"Building on variable selection, this node explains the actual algebraic transformations performed in each iteration of the Simplex method. Students will learn how to perform pivoting operations using elementary row operations to transform the tableau, making the entering variable basic and the leaving variable non-basic. This process systematically moves from one basic feasible solution to an adjacent one, improving the objective function value. The node emphasizes repeating these steps until the optimality condition is met, signifying that the optimal solution has been found.","status":"locked"},{"id":"interpreting_optimal_tableau_special_cases","title":"Interpreting the Optimal Tableau and Special Cases","description":"Upon reaching the optimal tableau, this node teaches students how to correctly extract the optimal values for all decision variables and the maximum (or minimum) value of the objective function. Furthermore, it covers how to identify and interpret special conditions that may arise during the Simplex process. Students will learn to recognize indicators for unbounded solutions, multiple optimal solutions, and degenerate solutions directly from the tableau, providing a comprehensive understanding of potential outcomes.","status":"locked"},{"id":"simplex_for_minimization","title":"Applying the Simplex Method to Minimization Problems","description":"This node extends the understanding of the Simplex method to solve minimization problems. Students will learn two primary approaches: either by transforming the minimization problem into an equivalent maximization problem by multiplying the objective function by -1, or by adjusting the optimality condition directly when using the original minimization objective. This ensures students can apply the powerful Simplex algorithm to a broader range of real-world linear programming scenarios, effectively handling both profit maximization and cost minimization objectives.","status":"locked"}]}
