{"nodes":[{"id":"ISSA001","title":"Interpreting Optimal Solutions","description":"This node focuses on accurately interpreting the numerical output from Linear Programming (LP) models. Students will learn to identify the optimal values for decision variables, which represent the quantities of products to produce or resources to allocate, and the optimal value of the objective function, indicating the maximum profit or minimum cost achievable. The goal is to translate these mathematical results into actionable business insights and understand what the 'best' solution means in a practical context.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ISSA002","title":"Understanding Slack and Surplus Variables","description":"This node explains the role and interpretation of slack and surplus variables within an LP solution. Slack variables represent the unused amount of a 'less than or equal to' resource constraint, indicating excess capacity. Conversely, surplus variables represent the amount by which a 'greater than or equal to' constraint is exceeded, often seen in minimum requirement scenarios. Understanding these helps in assessing resource utilization, identifying binding versus non-binding constraints, and ultimately improving operational efficiency.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ISSA003","title":"Introduction to Sensitivity Analysis","description":"This node introduces the fundamental concept of sensitivity analysis, also known as post-optimality analysis, and highlights its critical importance in managerial decision-making. Students will learn why it's essential to understand how robust an optimal solution is to changes in input parameters. Real-world scenarios often involve uncertainty, and sensitivity analysis provides insights into the range over which data inputs can vary without altering the optimal decision strategy or the structure of the optimal solution.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ISSA004","title":"Shadow Prices (Dual Prices)","description":"This node delves into the concept of shadow prices, also known as dual prices, which are crucial for evaluating resource constraints. A shadow price represents the change in the optimal objective function value for a one-unit increase in the right-hand side of a binding constraint, assuming all other parameters remain constant and the change is within the allowable range. Students will learn how to interpret shadow prices as the marginal value of an additional unit of a scarce resource, guiding decisions on resource acquisition or reallocation.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ISSA005","title":"Reduced Costs","description":"This node focuses on understanding and interpreting reduced costs, particularly for non-basic variables in an LP solution. For a non-basic variable (a product not produced or an activity not undertaken at the optimum), the reduced cost indicates how much its objective function coefficient (e.g., profit per unit) would need to improve before it becomes profitable to include that variable in the solution. For basic variables, the reduced cost is zero. Understanding reduced costs helps managers evaluate the viability of potential new products or alternative strategies.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ISSA006","title":"Ranges for Objective Function Coefficients","description":"This node explains how to interpret the allowable increase and decrease ranges for objective function coefficients. These ranges define how much the profit or cost coefficient of a decision variable can change without altering the set of optimal basic variables. In other words, they indicate how much the unit profit or cost can fluctuate without changing the optimal product mix or resource allocation, although the total objective function value will change. This analysis provides insights into the stability and robustness of the optimal strategy.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ISSA007","title":"Ranges for Right-Hand Side Values","description":"This node focuses on interpreting the allowable increase and decrease ranges for the right-hand side (RHS) values of constraints. These ranges indicate how much the availability of a resource (e.g., labor hours, raw materials) or a requirement (e.g., minimum production) can change without altering the shadow price of that constraint. Understanding these ranges is crucial for making informed decisions about acquiring additional resources or adjusting operational targets, as it defines the validity of the current shadow price and the associated marginal value.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ISSA008","title":"Managerial Implications and Decision Making","description":"This final node consolidates the knowledge gained from interpreting optimal solutions and sensitivity analysis into practical managerial insights. Students will learn how to synthesize information from shadow prices, reduced costs, and allowable ranges to make robust business decisions. This includes evaluating trade-offs, justifying resource acquisition, setting competitive prices, and understanding the strategic implications of potential changes in market conditions, resource availability, or internal capacities. The focus is on applying LP outputs to real-world problem-solving.","status":"locked"}]}
