{"nodes":[{"id":"管數ch2-node1","title":"Understanding Special Cases in Linear Programming","description":"This node introduces the concept of special cases in Linear Programming (LP), which deviate from the typical unique optimal solution. Students will learn about scenarios where LP models might yield no feasible solution, an infinitely large solution, or more than one optimal solution. Understanding these cases is crucial for accurately interpreting LP results and making informed managerial decisions, as they often indicate specific issues or characteristics within the problem formulation or the real-world situation it represents.","status":"locked"},{"id":"管數ch2-node2","title":"Identifying and Interpreting Infeasible LP Solutions","description":"This node focuses on infeasible solutions in Linear Programming. An infeasible solution occurs when no combination of decision variables can satisfy all the constraints simultaneously, meaning there is no feasible region. Students will learn how to identify infeasibility both graphically and through problem formulation, understanding that it often points to overly restrictive constraints or errors in model construction. We will discuss the practical implications of an infeasible model and strategies to revise the constraints to find a viable solution.","status":"locked"},{"id":"管數ch2-node3","title":"Recognizing and Addressing Unbounded LP Solutions","description":"This node explores unbounded solutions in Linear Programming. An unbounded solution arises when the objective function can be increased (for maximization problems) or decreased (for minimization problems) indefinitely without violating any constraints, meaning the feasible region extends infinitely in the direction of optimization. Students will learn to identify unboundedness graphically and understand its implications, typically indicating that one or more critical constraints have been omitted or incorrectly formulated in the model. We will discuss how to identify the missing constraints to properly bound the solution.","status":"locked"},{"id":"管數ch2-node4","title":"Exploring Multiple Optimal Solutions in LP","description":"This node delves into the scenario of multiple optimal solutions in Linear Programming. Multiple optimal solutions occur when the objective function line (or plane in higher dimensions) is parallel to one of the binding constraints, resulting in an entire segment or face of the feasible region yielding the same optimal objective function value. Students will learn to identify these cases graphically and understand that while the objective value is unique, there can be several different combinations of decision variable values that achieve this optimum. This understanding allows for greater flexibility in decision-making, as managers can choose among equally optimal solutions based on secondary criteria.","status":"locked"},{"id":"管數ch2-node5","title":"Understanding Degeneracy in Linear Programming","description":"This node introduces the concept of degeneracy in Linear Programming. Degeneracy occurs when a basic feasible solution has fewer than m positive basic variables (where m is the number of constraints), or when more than two constraint lines intersect at a single corner point of the feasible region in a two-variable problem. While degeneracy does not inherently prevent finding an optimal solution, it can cause issues in certain simplex algorithm implementations, potentially leading to cycling. Students will learn to recognize degenerate conditions and understand their theoretical implications, though practical solutions often involve minor perturbations.","status":"locked"},{"id":"管數ch2-node6","title":"Formulating and Understanding Transportation Problems","description":"This node introduces a classic application of Linear Programming: the Transportation Problem. Students will learn how to model scenarios involving the efficient distribution of goods from multiple sources (e.g., factories) to multiple destinations (e.g., warehouses) while minimizing total transportation costs. Key concepts include sources, destinations, supply capacities, demand requirements, and unit transportation costs. We will cover the standard LP formulation for transportation problems, setting the stage for various methods used to find an optimal distribution plan.","status":"locked"},{"id":"管數ch2-node7","title":"Methods for Solving Transportation Problems","description":"Building on the formulation, this node explores common methods for solving transportation problems. Students will learn about initial basic feasible solution methods such as the Northwest Corner Rule, Least Cost Method, and Vogel's Approximation Method. Furthermore, we will delve into iterative improvement techniques like the Stepping Stone Method or the Modified Distribution (MODI) Method, which are used to find the optimal transportation plan by evaluating unoccupied cells and reallocating shipments. The goal is to minimize total shipping costs while satisfying all supply and demand constraints.","status":"locked"},{"id":"管數ch2-node8","title":"Formulating and Understanding Assignment Problems","description":"This node introduces another specialized application of Linear Programming: the Assignment Problem. Students will learn how to model situations where various tasks need to be assigned to different resources (e.g., workers to jobs, machines to projects) in a one-to-one fashion, typically with the goal of minimizing total cost or maximizing total profit. Key concepts include tasks, resources, and the cost/profit associated with each possible assignment. We will cover the standard LP formulation for assignment problems, highlighting its unique structure as a special case of the transportation problem.","status":"locked"},{"id":"管數ch2-node9","title":"Applying the Hungarian Method for Assignment Problems","description":"This node focuses on the most efficient algorithm for solving assignment problems: the Hungarian Method. Students will learn the step-by-step procedure of this method, which involves transforming the cost matrix through row and column reductions to identify optimal assignments. The method ensures that each resource is assigned to exactly one task and each task is assigned to exactly one resource, leading to the minimum total cost or maximum total profit. Practical examples will illustrate how to apply the Hungarian Method to various assignment scenarios.","status":"locked"},{"id":"管數ch2-node10","title":"Advanced LP Applications and Strategic Managerial Insights","description":"This concluding node synthesizes the learning from special cases and advanced applications, providing a broader perspective on LP's utility. Students will review how understanding special cases like infeasibility or unboundedness can prevent misinterpretations and lead to better model refinement. We will also discuss how transportation and assignment models can be extended to more complex real-world scenarios, such as facility location, scheduling, and project management. The node emphasizes the strategic role of LP as a decision-making tool, enabling managers to optimize resource allocation and gain competitive advantages.","status":"locked"}]}
