ts. Crucially, the node will highlight that matrix multiplication is generally not commutative (AB ≠ BA) but is associative ((AB)C = A(BC)) and distributive (A(B+C) = AB + AC). Mastering matrix multiplication is vital for solving systems of linear equations and understanding linear transformations.","status":"locked"},{"id":"identity_matrix","title":"單位矩陣","description":"This node introduces the identity matrix, denoted as I or Iₙ, which plays a role similar to the number '1' in scalar multiplication. Students will learn that an identity matrix is a square matrix with ones on its main diagonal and zeros everywhere else. A key property explored is that multiplying any matrix A by the identity matrix I (when dimensions allow) results in the original matrix A, i.e., AI = IA = A. Understanding the identity matrix is fundamental for concepts like inverse matrices and matrix transformations.","status":"locked"}]}
