{"nodes":[{"id":"node_1_linear_systems_matrix_rep","title":"Representing Linear Systems with Matrices","description":"This node introduces how linear systems of equations can be concisely represented using matrices in the form Ax = b. Students will learn to identify the coefficient matrix A, the variable vector x, and the constant vector b. We will also cover the concept of an augmented matrix [A|b] as a compact way to represent the entire system, and briefly touch upon the three fundamental possibilities for the nature of solutions: a unique solution, no solution, or infinitely many solutions.","status":"locked"},{"id":"node_2_gaussian_elimination_ref","title":"Gaussian Elimination and Row Echelon Form","description":"This node focuses on Gaussian elimination, the primary algorithm for systematically solving linear systems. We will detail the three elementary row operations: swapping two rows, multiplying a row by a non-zero scalar, and adding a multiple of one row to another. The main objective of Gaussian elimination is to transform the augmented matrix into row echelon form, where students will learn to identify pivots (the first non-zero element in each row) and understand the characteristic 'staircase' pattern of this form.","status":"locked"},{"id":"node_3_interpreting_solutions","title":"Interpreting Solutions from Row Echelon Form","description":"Upon achieving row echelon form, this node teaches how to interpret the resulting matrix to determine the nature of the linear system's solutions. Students will learn to distinguish between systems with a unique solution (when every variable corresponds to a pivot), no solution (indicated by a row like [0 0 ... 0 | b] where b ≠ 0), and infinite solutions. The concept of 'free variables,' which correspond to non-pivot columns and can take any value, will be explained as the source of infinite solutions, leading to parametric solution forms.","status":"locked"},{"id":"node_4_elementary_permutation_matrices","title":"Elementary and Permutation Matrices","description":"This node introduces elementary matrices, which are obtained by performing a single elementary row operation on an identity matrix. Students will learn the three types of elementary matrices corresponding to the three row operations and understand their crucial property of being invertible. We will also cover permutation matrices as a special type of elementary matrix, characterized by having exactly one '1' in each row and column, and explain their role in performing row exchanges, noting the property P⁻¹ = Pᵀ.","status":"locked"},{"id":"node_5_matrix_decompositions_lu_plu","title":"Matrix Decompositions: LU and PLU","description":"This node explores matrix decompositions, starting with LU decomposition (A=LU), where A is factored into a lower triangular matrix L and an upper triangular matrix U. Students will understand how this decomposition can significantly streamline the process of solving multiple linear systems with the same coefficient matrix A. We will also address the limitations of LU decomposition (requiring no row swaps) and introduce the more general PLU decomposition (A=PLU), which incorporates a permutation matrix P to account for necessary row exchanges, making it applicable to a wider range of matrices.","status":"locked"},{"id":"node_6_finding_inverse_matrices","title":"Finding Inverse Matrices using Gaussian Elimination","description":"This node details the method for finding the inverse of a square matrix A using Gaussian elimination. Students will learn the process of augmenting the matrix A with an identity matrix I, forming [A|I], and then applying elementary row operations to transform the left side into the identity matrix, simultaneously transforming the right side into A⁻¹. We will discuss the condition for a matrix to be invertible (it must be reducible to the identity matrix) and how an invertible matrix can be expressed as a product of elementary matrices.","status":"locked"},{"id":"node_7_homogeneous_general_solutions","title":"Homogeneous Systems and General Solutions","description":"This node defines homogeneous linear systems as those in the form Ax=0 and explores their solutions. Students will learn that every homogeneous system always has at least the trivial solution (x=0). We will then introduce the concept of a general solution for any linear system Ax=b, which is expressed as the sum of a particular solution (xₚ) and the solution to the corresponding homogeneous system (xₕ), i.e., x = xₚ + xₕ. The presence of free variables in xₕ will be shown to lead to infinitely many solutions for homogeneous systems.","status":"locked"},{"id":"node_8_linear_dependence_theorems","title":"Linear Dependence, Independence, and Key Theorems","description":"This node defines linear dependence and independence of vectors, explaining that vectors are linearly dependent if a non-trivial linear combination yields the zero vector, and linearly independent otherwise. Students will learn to test for dependence/independence by solving the homogeneous system Ax=0. We will also summarize several important theorems related to invertible matrices, such as the fact that the product of invertible matrices is invertible, the property for the inverse of a product (AB)⁻¹ = B⁻¹A⁻¹, and the relationship between an invertible matrix and the linear independence of its column vectors.","status":"locked"}]}
