{"nodes":[{"id":"matrix_basics","title":"Essential Matrix Types and Elementary Operations","description":"This node introduces fundamental matrix types such as diagonal, upper triangular, lower triangular, and symmetric matrices, which are crucial components in matrix decompositions. Students will also learn about elementary matrices, derived from identity matrices through single row operations, and their role in transforming matrices. Additionally, the concept of permutation matrices, used for row exchanges, and their unique property (P⁻¹ = Pᵀ) will be covered, laying the groundwork for understanding matrix factorization.","status":"locked"},{"id":"gaussian_elimination","title":"Linear Systems and Gaussian Elimination","description":"This node focuses on representing linear systems in the form Ax = b and understanding the possibilities of unique, no, or infinite solutions. Students will learn the process of Gaussian elimination, a systematic method using elementary row operations (swapping rows, scaling rows, adding multiples of rows) to transform an augmented matrix [A|b] into row echelon form. Key concepts like pivots and free variables will be defined, illustrating how this method provides a foundational approach to solving systems and prepares for matrix decompositions.","status":"locked"},{"id":"lu_decomposition","title":"LU Decomposition: Factoring for Efficiency","description":"This node delves into the LU decomposition, where a matrix A is factored into a lower triangular matrix L and an upper triangular matrix U (A = LU). Students will understand the structure of L and U and the conditions under which an LU decomposition exists. The primary advantage of LU decomposition for solving linear systems (Ax=b by solving Ly=b and then Ux=y) will be explored, highlighting how it significantly enhances computational efficiency for multiple right-hand side vectors. This node also addresses the limitations of LU decomposition, setting the stage for more generalized methods.","status":"locked"},{"id":"plu_decomposition","title":"PLU Decomposition: Generalizing Factorization","description":"Building on the LU decomposition, this node introduces the PLU decomposition (A = PLU), which incorporates a permutation matrix P to handle situations where row exchanges are necessary during the factorization process. Students will learn why PLU is more general and robust than LU decomposition, especially for matrices that require pivoting. The role of the permutation matrix P in reordering rows before applying LU factorization will be explained, demonstrating how it ensures that a decomposition can always be found for invertible matrices.","status":"locked"},{"id":"ldu_decomposition","title":"LDU Decomposition: Detailed Matrix Structure","description":"This node explores the LDU decomposition (A = LDU), a more detailed factorization compared to LU. Here, L is a lower triangular matrix with ones on its diagonal, U is an upper triangular matrix with ones on its diagonal, and D is a diagonal matrix containing the pivots. Students will understand how LDU provides a finer structural insight into the original matrix A, separating the scaling factors into D and leaving the \"pure\" triangular structures in L and U. This decomposition is particularly useful for symmetric matrices, where L = Uᵀ, simplifying analysis.","status":"locked"},{"id":"invertibility_linear_dependence","title":"Matrix Invertibility and Linear Independence","description":"This node connects matrix decompositions to the broader concepts of matrix invertibility and linear dependence. Students will learn how to determine if a matrix is invertible using Gaussian elimination to transform [A|I] into [I|A⁻¹], and understand that invertible matrices can be expressed as a product of elementary matrices. The concepts of linear independence and dependence among vectors will be defined, along with methods to test for them (e.g., solving Ax=0). This node consolidates understanding by linking matrix properties to the existence and nature of solutions to linear systems.","status":"locked"}]}
