{"nodes":[{"id":"advanced_matrix_01","title":"Gaussian Elimination and Row Echelon Form","description":"This node introduces Gaussian elimination as a fundamental method for transforming matrices into Row Echelon Form. Students will learn the three basic row operations: swapping rows, multiplying a row by a non-zero scalar, and adding a multiple of one row to another. The goal is to understand how to systematically reduce a matrix to a form where zero rows are at the bottom, pivots descend from left to right, and elements below pivots are zero. This process is crucial for solving linear systems, finding inverse matrices, and determining linear dependence.","status":"locked"},{"id":"advanced_matrix_02","title":"Methods for Finding Inverse Matrices","description":"This node delves into the primary method for calculating the inverse of a square matrix, A⁻¹. Students will learn to use Gaussian elimination by augmenting the matrix A with an identity matrix I, forming [A|I]. The process involves performing row operations to transform [A|I] into [I|B], where B then represents A⁻¹. Additionally, the concept of representing an inverse matrix as a product of elementary matrices will be explored, providing a deeper understanding of matrix invertibility and its properties.","status":"locked"},{"id":"advanced_matrix_03","title":"Elementary and Permutation Matrices","description":"This node explores elementary matrices, which are formed by performing a single elementary row operation on an identity matrix. Students will learn about the three types of elementary matrices corresponding to row swaps, scalar multiplication of a row, and adding a multiple of one row to another. Furthermore, the concept of permutation matrices, which are used specifically for row exchanges and have the property P⁻¹ = Pᵀ, will be covered. Understanding these special matrices is essential for comprehending matrix invertibility and advanced matrix decompositions like PLU.","status":"locked"},{"id":"advanced_matrix_04","title":"Linear Dependence and Independence","description":"This node introduces the critical concepts of linear dependence and independence among a set of vectors. Students will learn that vectors are linearly dependent if a non-trivial linear combination (where not all coefficients are zero) results in the zero vector. Conversely, vectors are linearly independent if only the trivial linear combination (all coefficients are zero) yields the zero vector. The primary method for testing linear dependence involves setting up and solving the homogeneous system Ax = 0, where the columns of A are the vectors in question.","status":"locked"},{"id":"advanced_matrix_05","title":"Homogeneous Systems and Trivial Solutions","description":"This node focuses on homogeneous linear systems, which are always of the form Ax = 0. Students will understand that such systems always have at least the trivial solution (x = 0). The core objective is to determine whether a homogeneous system possesses non-trivial solutions, which are solutions where not all components of x are zero. The existence of non-trivial solutions is directly linked to the linear dependence of the column vectors of A and the presence of free variables in the system's solution.","status":"locked"},{"id":"advanced_matrix_06","title":"Free Variables and General Solutions","description":"This node explains the concept of free variables within a linear system, which correspond to columns in the row echelon form of the matrix that do not contain a pivot. Students will learn that free variables can take on any arbitrary value, leading to infinitely many solutions for systems that possess them. This node also covers how to express the general solution of a linear system, including the structure x = xₚ + xₕ, where xₚ is a particular solution and xₕ is the solution to the associated homogeneous system, often parameterized by free variables.","status":"locked"},{"id":"advanced_matrix_07","title":"LU and PLU Decompositions","description":"This node introduces advanced matrix factorizations, starting with LU decomposition, where a matrix A is expressed as the product of a lower triangular matrix L and an upper triangular matrix U. Students will understand the benefits of LU decomposition for efficiently solving linear systems. Furthermore, the more general PLU decomposition will be covered, which accounts for row exchanges by introducing a permutation matrix P, allowing for the factorization A = PLU even when LU decomposition alone is not possible due to zero pivots.","status":"locked"},{"id":"advanced_matrix_08","title":"LDU Decomposition and Properties of Invertible Matrices","description":"This node explores the LDU decomposition, a more refined factorization where A = LDU, with L and U being lower and upper triangular matrices respectively (both with 1s on the diagonal), and D being a diagonal matrix. Students will learn how this decomposition provides a deeper insight into a matrix's structure. Additionally, this node will summarize important theorems related to invertible matrices, such as the invertibility of a product (AB)⁻¹ = B⁻¹A⁻¹, the property that symmetric matrices have L=Uᵀ in LDU decomposition, and the fundamental connection between matrix invertibility and linearly independent column vectors.","status":"locked"}]}
